Chapter: OceanRender
  cites Water chapter OceanModel

 The ocean model, drawn. `OceanModel` samples the surface at four points
 and prints them; four numbers are enough to check that a normal is unit
 and that foam appears where the Jacobian folds, and they are not enough
 to see whether the thing looks like water. This chapter evaluates the
 same model over a grid, projects the displaced points through a camera,
 and fills the triangles into the GOP framebuffer, so the output is an
 image rather than a reading.

 Nothing here changes the model. Every surface point still comes from
 `eval-surface` and every colour still comes from `shade-point`. What is
 added is a camera, a scanline filler, and distance haze.

 Three choices worth stating, because each had an alternative.

 The framebuffer is written directly with `poke-32` rather than through
 `Game chapter Rasterizer`. That chapter carries the same scanline math
 and is the obvious thing to cite, but its `Framebuf` is a `List Integer`
 and `fb-set` builds a fresh `Framebuf` record for every pixel it writes.
 On a heap with no collector that is a record per pixel for a quarter of
 a million pixels, to produce an intermediate that would then have to be
 copied into the framebuffer anyway. The filler below is the same
 algorithm without the copy or the garbage.

 There is no depth buffer. The grid is drawn one band at a time from the
 far edge toward the camera, which is a painter's ordering and is exact
 for a heightfield. Gerstner displacement can fold a crest over the
 trough in front of it, and where it does the near band wins by drawing
 later, which is the wrong answer for those pixels. It costs a few pixels
 at a crest lip and it saves sorting every triangle.

 The wave list is bound once in `opening` and threaded through every
 call. A list constant is rebuilt at each mention, so naming
 `ocean-waves` inside the per-vertex path would rebuild four `Wave`
 records for every vertex on the grid.

Section: Framebuffer

 The standing GOP surface codex-vm renders at 0xBF000000: one 32-bit XRGB
 pixel per cell, row-major, no pitch padding. A write outside the bounds
 is dropped rather than wrapped. The resolution here must match the
 `-gop-width` and `-gop-height` the machine is booted with; the sidecar
 in the README passes both.

  or-fb-base : Integer
  or-fb-base = #BF000000

  or-width : Integer
  or-width = 640

  or-height : Integer
  or-height = 400

  or-half-w : Real
  or-half-w = 320.0

  or-half-h : Real
  or-half-h = 200.0

  or-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  or-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  or-pack : Real, Real, Real -> Integer
  or-pack (r) (g) (b) =
    let ri = real-to-int (w-sat r * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let gi = real-to-int (w-sat g * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let bi = real-to-int (w-sat b * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in ri * 65536 + gi * 256 + bi

  or-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-put (x) (y) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= or-width then 0
    else if y >= or-height then 0
    else poke-32 or-fb-base ((y * or-width + x) * 4) color

  or-span : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-span (x) (x1) (y) (color) =
    if x > x1 then 0
    else let d = or-put x y color
    in or-span (x + 1) x1 y color

Section: Sky

 A vertical gradient, dark at the zenith and pale at the bottom, so the
 water has something to fog into and the image has a horizon. The water
 is drawn over the lower part of it.

  or-sky-zenith : V3
  or-sky-zenith = V3 { x = 0.26, y = 0.45, z = 0.72 }

  or-sky-haze : V3
  or-sky-haze = V3 { x = 0.72, y = 0.82, z = 0.9 }

  or-sky-at : Integer -> Integer
  or-sky-at (y) =
    let t = real-from-int y / real-from-int (or-height - 1)
    in let c = v-lerp or-sky-zenith or-sky-haze t
    in or-pack c.x c.y c.z

  or-clear : Integer -> Integer
  or-clear (y) =
    if y >= or-height then 0
    else let d = or-span 0 (or-width - 1) y (or-sky-at y)
    in or-clear (y + 1)

Section: Camera

 A look-at frame built from the eye the model already carries. Right is
 the cross product of world up with forward, which is what puts world +x
 on the right of the image for a camera looking along +z; up is then
 forward crossed with right. A point is projected by measuring it along
 the three axes and dividing the two lateral components by the forward
 one.

  v-cross : V3, V3 -> V3
  v-cross (a) (b) = V3 {
    x = a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y,
    y = a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z,
    z = a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x
  }

  Cam = record { cf : V3, cr : V3, cu : V3 }

  or-world-up : V3
  or-world-up = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }

  or-target : V3
  or-target = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 0.0, z = 22.0 }

  or-focal : Real
  or-focal = 520.0

  or-near : Real
  or-near = 0.5

  or-camera : Cam
  or-camera =
    let f = v-norm (v-sub or-target ocean-eye)
    in let r = v-norm (v-cross or-world-up f)
    in Cam { cf = f, cr = r, cu = v-cross f r }

Section: Distance Haze

 Aerial perspective. Without it the grid ends in a hard line across the
 image where the last band stops, which reads as a cut sheet rather than
 a sea. Fogging the far water into the same colour the sky reaches at the
 horizon hides the edge for the same reason it does outdoors.

  or-fog-start : Real
  or-fog-start = 35.0

  or-fog-end : Real
  or-fog-end = 145.0

  or-fog : V3, Real -> V3
  or-fog (c) (dist) =
    let t = w-sat ((dist - or-fog-start) / (or-fog-end - or-fog-start))
    in v-lerp c or-sky-haze t

Section: Vertices

 One grid vertex: where the displaced surface point lands on screen,
 whether it is in front of the camera at all, and the colour the model
 shades it. Colour is carried per vertex and averaged per triangle, so a
 facet takes the mean of its three corners rather than one sample.

  Vtx = record {
    vx : Real,
    vy : Real,
    vok : Integer,
    vr : Real,
    vg : Real,
    vb : Real
  }

  or-vtx-behind : Vtx
  or-vtx-behind = Vtx { vx = 0.0, vy = 0.0, vok = 0, vr = 0.0, vg = 0.0, vb = 0.0 }

  or-vtx-visible : Surface, Cam, V3, Real -> Vtx
  or-vtx-visible (s) (cam) (d) (cz) =
    let cx = v-dot d (cam.cr)
    in let cy = v-dot d (cam.cu)
    in let lit = or-fog (shade-point s ocean-eye ocean-sun) cz
    in Vtx {
      vx = or-half-w + cx * or-focal / cz,
      vy = or-half-h - cy * or-focal / cz,
      vok = 1,
      vr = lit.x,
      vg = lit.y,
      vb = lit.z
    }

  or-vertex : List Wave, Cam, Real, Real -> Vtx
  or-vertex (ws) (cam) (x) (z) =
    let s = eval-surface ws x z ocean-time
    in let p = V3 { x = s.px, y = s.py, z = s.pz }
    in let d = v-sub p ocean-eye
    in let cz = v-dot d (cam.cf)
    in if cz < or-near then or-vtx-behind else or-vtx-visible s cam d cz

Section: The Grid

 The grid is shaped like the view frustum, not like a rectangle of water.

 A rectangular slab is the obvious thing to sample and it is wrong here,
 for a reason worth writing down because the picture it produces does not
 look like a domain problem. Fix the width and the near rows are as wide
 as the far ones while sitting a few units from the eye, so they project
 to thousands of pixels across and arrive as slivers spanning the screen,
 while the far rows shrink below a pixel and contribute nothing. The
 first render of this chapter was that image.

 Making each row's half-width proportional to its distance ahead of the
 eye gives every row roughly the same width on screen and every quad
 roughly the same screen area, which is where the samples are worth
 spending. The margin carries the row edges past the frame so the sides
 are covered.

 Rows are spaced quadratically in depth, so the near water, which fills
 most of the image, gets most of the rows.

  or-nx : Integer
  or-nx = 120

  or-nz : Integer
  or-nz = 90

  or-z0 : Real
  or-z0 = 0.0 - 26.0

  or-z1 : Real
  or-z1 = 260.0

  or-margin : Real
  or-margin = 1.8

  or-half-width-at : Real -> Real
  or-half-width-at (z) =
    let ahead = z - ocean-eye.z
    in let d = if ahead < 1.0 then 1.0 else ahead
    in d * or-half-w / or-focal * or-margin

  or-x-at : Real, Integer -> Real
  or-x-at (z) (i) =
    let hw = or-half-width-at z
    in ocean-eye.x - hw + 2.0 * hw * real-from-int i / real-from-int or-nx

  or-z-at : Integer -> Real
  or-z-at (j) =
    let t = real-from-int j / real-from-int or-nz
    in or-z0 + (or-z1 - or-z0) * t * t

  or-row : List Wave, Cam, Integer, Real, List Vtx -> List Vtx
  or-row (ws) (cam) (i) (z) (acc) =
    if i > or-nx then acc
    else or-row ws cam (i + 1) z (list-push acc (or-vertex ws cam (or-x-at z i) z))

Section: Wavelength Level of Detail

 A frustum-shaped grid holds the screen density constant, which means the
 world-space spacing between samples grows without bound with distance.
 The far rows of this grid sit 4 units apart and the shortest wave in the
 set is 3.8 units long, so out there the wave field is sampled below its
 Nyquist rate. An undersampled sine does not fade out, it returns an
 arbitrary phase per vertex, and the model faithfully reports what those
 phases imply: gradients that are nonsense, a Jacobian that folds, and
 foam. The first frustum render came back 99 per cent foam-white for
 exactly this reason, and nothing was wrong with the model.

 So each row gets its own copy of the wave set with the waves it cannot
 resolve attenuated out. A wave is at full strength when its wavelength
 is four or more times the row spacing and gone by twice it, faded rather
 than dropped so that a wave does not pop as a row crosses the threshold.
 Amplitude and steepness are attenuated together, which keeps the crest
 sharpening in proportion to the height it sharpens.

 This is the same thing a mip-mapped displacement map does on a GPU, and
 the same thing an eye does: distant water is smooth because its small
 structure is below the resolution looking at it.

  or-spacing-at : Real -> Real
  or-spacing-at (z) = 2.0 * or-half-width-at z / real-from-int or-nx

  or-lod-wave : Wave, Real -> Wave
  or-lod-wave (w) (spacing) =
    let f = w-sat ((w.wavelen / spacing - 2.0) / 2.0)
    in Wave {
      dx = w.dx,
      dy = w.dy,
      amp = w.amp * f,
      wavelen = w.wavelen,
      speed = w.speed,
      steep = w.steep * f
    }

  or-lod-waves : List Wave, Real, Integer, List Wave -> List Wave
  or-lod-waves (ws) (spacing) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ws then acc
    else or-lod-waves ws spacing (i + 1) (list-push acc (or-lod-wave (list-at ws i) spacing))

  or-row-at : List Wave, Cam, Integer -> List Vtx
  or-row-at (ws) (cam) (j) =
    let z = or-z-at j
    in or-row (or-lod-waves ws (or-spacing-at z) 0 []) cam 0 z []

Section: Triangle Fill

 Scanline fill of a screen triangle, sorted by y and swept in two halves
 across the long edge. The same shape as the one in `Game chapter
 Rasterizer`, writing pixels instead of building a framebuffer record.

  Tri3 = record {
    tax : Integer, tay : Integer,
    tbx : Integer, tby : Integer,
    tcx : Integer, tcy : Integer
  }

  or-sort3 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Tri3
  or-sort3 (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (x2) (y2) =
    if y0 <= y1 then
      if y1 <= y2 then Tri3 { tax = x0, tay = y0, tbx = x1, tby = y1, tcx = x2, tcy = y2 }
      else if y0 <= y2 then Tri3 { tax = x0, tay = y0, tbx = x2, tby = y2, tcx = x1, tcy = y1 }
      else Tri3 { tax = x2, tay = y2, tbx = x0, tby = y0, tcx = x1, tcy = y1 }
    else if y0 <= y2 then Tri3 { tax = x1, tay = y1, tbx = x0, tby = y0, tcx = x2, tcy = y2 }
    else if y1 <= y2 then Tri3 { tax = x1, tay = y1, tbx = x2, tby = y2, tcx = x0, tcy = y0 }
    else Tri3 { tax = x2, tay = y2, tbx = x1, tby = y1, tcx = x0, tcy = y0 }

  or-edge-x : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-edge-x (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (y) =
    let dy = y1 - y0
    in if dy == 0 then x0
    else x0 + (x1 - x0) * (y - y0) / dy

  or-half : Tri3, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-half (t) (y-start) (y-end) (color) =
    if y-start > y-end then 0
    else if y-start >= or-height then 0
    else or-half-step t y-start y-end color

  or-half-step : Tri3, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-half-step (t) (y) (y-end) (color) =
    let xac = or-edge-x (t.tax) (t.tay) (t.tcx) (t.tcy) y
    in let xside = if y < t.tby then or-edge-x (t.tax) (t.tay) (t.tbx) (t.tby) y
                   else or-edge-x (t.tbx) (t.tby) (t.tcx) (t.tcy) y
    in let lo = or-max (or-min xac xside) 0
    in let hi = or-min (or-max xac xside) (or-width - 1)
    in let d = if y >= 0 then or-span lo hi y color else 0
    in or-half t (y + 1) y-end color

  or-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-fill (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (x2) (y2) (color) =
    let t = or-sort3 x0 y0 x1 y1 x2 y2
    in let d = or-half t (t.tay) (t.tby) color
    in or-half t (t.tby) (t.tcy) color

Section: Drawing the Bands

 A facet is drawn only when all three of its corners are in front of the
 camera. A partially visible triangle is dropped rather than clipped: the
 grid starts well in front of the eye, so the only facets this discards
 are behind the camera entirely.

  or-tri : Vtx, Vtx, Vtx -> Integer
  or-tri (a) (b) (c) =
    if a.vok + b.vok + c.vok < 3 then 0
    else or-tri-draw a b c

  or-tri-draw : Vtx, Vtx, Vtx -> Integer
  or-tri-draw (a) (b) (c) =
    let col = or-pack ((a.vr + b.vr + c.vr) / 3.0) ((a.vg + b.vg + c.vg) / 3.0) ((a.vb + b.vb + c.vb) / 3.0)
    in or-fill (real-to-int a.vx) (real-to-int a.vy) (real-to-int b.vx) (real-to-int b.vy) (real-to-int c.vx) (real-to-int c.vy) col

  or-band : List Vtx, List Vtx, Integer -> Integer
  or-band (far) (near) (i) =
    if i >= or-nx then 0
    else or-band-cell far near i

  or-band-cell : List Vtx, List Vtx, Integer -> Integer
  or-band-cell (far) (near) (i) =
    let a = list-at far i
    in let b = list-at far (i + 1)
    in let c = list-at near i
    in let e = list-at near (i + 1)
    in let t1 = or-tri a b c
    in let t2 = or-tri b e c
    in or-band far near (i + 1)

  or-rows : List Wave, Cam, Integer, List Vtx -> Integer
  or-rows (ws) (cam) (j) (far) =
    if j < 0 then 0
    else or-rows-step ws cam j far

  or-rows-step : List Wave, Cam, Integer, List Vtx -> Integer
  or-rows-step (ws) (cam) (j) (far) =
    let near = or-row-at ws cam j
    in let d = or-band far near 0
    in or-rows ws cam (j - 1) near

Section: Reading the Result Back

 The image is the deliverable, but a run that only paints tells you
 nothing on a terminal. These two counts are read back out of the
 framebuffer after the draw, and both are numbers that move when the
 render breaks: covered counts pixels that no longer hold the sky
 gradient their row was cleared to, so it goes to zero if the mesh fails
 to draw, and foam counts near-white pixels, which is the model's own
 Jacobian criterion arriving on screen.

  or-is-foam : Integer -> Integer
  or-is-foam (px) =
    let r = int-mod (px / 65536) 256
    in let g = int-mod (px / 256) 256
    in let b = int-mod px 256
    in if r >= 217 then (if g >= 217 then (if b >= 217 then 1 else 0) else 0) else 0

  or-scan-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-scan-row (y) (x) (sky) (want-foam) (acc) =
    if x >= or-width then acc
    else or-scan-row y (x + 1) sky want-foam (acc + or-scan-px (peek-32 or-fb-base ((y * or-width + x) * 4)) sky want-foam)

  or-scan-px : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-scan-px (px) (sky) (want-foam) =
    if want-foam == 1 then or-is-foam px
    else if px == sky then 0
    else 1

  or-scan : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-scan (y) (want-foam) (acc) =
    if y >= or-height then acc
    else or-scan (y + 1) want-foam (acc + or-scan-row y 0 (or-sky-at y) want-foam 0)

Section: Holding the Frame

 codex-vm captures the framebuffer on a timer while the guest is still
 running, so a program that draws and exits inside a second is already
 gone when the capture fires. The driver holds after drawing.

 The hold counts PIT ticks at cell 28672 rather than spinning a fixed
 number of iterations, so it is a duration and not a guess about how fast
 this machine retires instructions. The fuel cap is not decoration: on a
 machine booted with the timer disabled the tick never advances, and
 without the cap the hold would be permanent.

 In a run that takes a screenshot the capture ends the guest partway
 through this hold, so the last line the driver prints does not appear.
 That absence is the capture working.

  or-tick-addr : Integer
  or-tick-addr = 28672

  or-hold-ticks : Integer
  or-hold-ticks = 90

  or-hold-fuel : Integer
  or-hold-fuel = 40000000000

  or-hold : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  or-hold (until) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if peek-32 or-tick-addr 0 >= until then 1
    else or-hold until (fuel - 1)

  or-hold-frame : Integer
  or-hold-frame = or-hold (peek-32 or-tick-addr 0 + or-hold-ticks) or-hold-fuel

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "Ocean render (Gerstner grid, painter bands, GGX + SSS shading)"
    print-line-uni ("resolution = " & show or-width & "x" & show or-height)
    print-line-uni ("grid = " & show or-nx & "x" & show or-nz & " cells, time = " & show ocean-time)
    let ws = ocean-waves
    in let cam = or-camera
    in let c0 = or-clear 0
    in let d0 = or-rows ws cam (or-nz - 1) (or-row-at ws cam or-nz)
    in let cov = or-scan 0 0 0
    in let fm = or-scan 0 1 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("covered = " & show cov)
      print-line-uni ("foam = " & show fm)
      print-line-uni ("held = " & show or-hold-frame)
    end
  end
